Tranche 2 Deliverable 3: cash to USDC to agUSD and the reverse, on Stellar
testnet, against the SDF Stellar Test Anchor.

The award's criterion names this anchor as covering these flows during MoneyGram
onboarding. Every line below is a transaction anyone can open.

  anchor            testanchor.stellar.org
  account           GC35EAQJPYWZDPOUMOOLGETVTDMG3KQDKOX4ISGOLXS2RR4JLLLGBFF2
  asset             USDC:GBBD47IF6LWK7P7MDEVSCWR7DPUWV3NY3DTQEVFL4NAT4AQH3ZLLFLA5
                    which is the asset the Vault custodies: the SAC of that
                    issuer is CBIELTK6YBZJU5UP2WWQEUCYKLPU6AUNZ2BQ4WWFEIE3USCIHMXQDAMA,
                    the contracts.usdc of this deployment. Checked with
                    `stellar contract id asset`, not assumed.

== 1. SEP-10

  challenge from    GCHLHDBOKG2JWMJQBTLSL5XG6NO7ESXI2TAQKZXCXWXB5WI2X6W233PR
  verified          source is the toml's SIGNING_KEY, sequence 0, first op a
                    ManageData naming this account, home domain matched, server
                    signature valid, time bounds current
  JWT               sub = the account, exp +24h

== 2. SEP-24 deposit, cash to USDC

  transaction       ae2734cc-2458-419f-8910-d3f53d04be71
  the form          completed in a browser. That is what interactive means in
                    SEP-24: the anchor serves its own webview and collects what
                    its jurisdiction requires.
  amount_in         5 USD
  fee               0.5 USD
  amount_out        4.5 USDC
  status            completed
  stellar tx        4a2167a714fee32dfa0463b70c73131ee866a336a084f19c7d92cedba9f5a439

== 3. USDC to agUSD, one for one, through the Vault

  trustline         agUSD:GAXM76GV3KKUK4XC3DKKHBEXCGUPYOALOB35HITI4WBKAAC7MUT5ZZEB
                    83aef2a504aa7e8d1339e3190e97f6aeb8c2f696eab6d2186c0097ff1a44e959
                    agUSD is a classic asset, so the account needs a trustline
                    before the Vault can mint to it. Without one the mint fails
                    with "trustline entry is missing for account", which is the
                    SAC refusing rather than the Vault.
  Vault::deposit    transfer 45000000 USDC to the Vault
                    mint     45000000 agUSD to the holder
                    Deposit  amount 45000000 minted 45000000
  agUSD balance     45000000

== 4. agUSD back to USDC, through the withdrawal queue

  request_withdrawal  burn 45000000 agUSD
                      WithdrawalRequested claim_id 54 queue_position 0
  claim_status        Ready
  claim_withdrawal    transfer 45000000 USDC from the Vault to the holder
                      WithdrawalClaimed claim_id 54 amount 45000000

== 5. SEP-24 withdrawal, USDC to cash

  transaction       57eaf7e1-2ce1-410d-9396-84969f227568
  the form          completed in a browser, bank and account as placeholders
  amount_in         4.5 USDC
  fee               0.4 USDC
  amount_out        4.1 USD
  anchor account    GBN4NNCDGJO4XW4KQU3CBIESUJWFVBUZPOKUZHT7W7WRB7CWOA7BXVQF
  memo              id:6100031375387207126
                    both read off the anchor's own transaction record, not
                    hardcoded: a withdrawal paid to a hardcoded address is a
                    withdrawal paid to whatever that address was last time
  payment           994d83972c03439c02dc27eba3f4ab3b84e365f748fb27130102ae95c8bb59c2
  status            pending_anchor "Received an incoming payment"
                    then completed

The round trip is closed. 5 USD of cash became 4.5 USDC became 4.5 agUSD, came
back through the FIFO queue as 4.5 USDC, and left as 4.1 USD of cash. The 0.9
USD of difference is the anchor's two fees, 0.5 on the way in and 0.4 on the way
out, and they are the test anchor's numbers rather than anything this protocol
charges: the Vault minted and redeemed one for one in both directions.
